1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:24,240
The school index has eleven Week 13 rows: autonomic and acid-base
review; all clinical pathways; all treatment protocols; valvular
disease and murmurs; patient autonomy; medication-safety framework;
implementation and evaluation; systems-based practice; antiemetics;
self-determination and advocacy; and blood-pressure interpretation.

2
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:45,720
Nancy Caroline Canadian eighth edition remains the learner-confirmed
principal examination text. The weekly map points to Chapter 48
chronic-care assessment and home-health headings, Chapters 34 and 35
for GI and renal/urologic context, and Chapters 18 and 19 for
decisions and documentation.

3
00:00:45,720 --> 00:01:11,760
All eleven assigned readers now have source-specific English and
Chinese explanations, with the identified files and review extent
recorded. This is not merely a list of candidate textbooks. Repeated
ranges retain previously checked teaching; newly assigned boundaries
and the Week Thirteen applications were reviewed separately. Open each
reader for the connected explanation, not just its chapter heading.

4
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:41,232
Real differences remain explicit. The CPSC literal pages discuss
drains, so the named medication-safety framework is taught alongside
them. The identified school cardiovascular book has the named valve
chapter, but not the printed page range in the school table. We teach
that complete named chapter without claiming the unresolved pages
match. Source-specific limits and current practice comparisons remain
visible in the bilingual readers.

5
00:01:41,232 --> 00:01:55,320
For a mechanism chapter, connect the idea to an observable patient
finding and name a competing explanation. For a procedure or
medication chapter, write the safety check and provider-level boundary
beside the concept.

6
00:01:55,320 --> 00:02:09,432
For every assigned page, keep the page locator in your notes and mark
what you personally reviewed. A course summary is not evidence that an
assigned figure, table, linked video or entire page range has been
completed.

7
00:02:09,432 --> 00:02:29,688
The school plan asks you to learn about PEG/NG and urinary devices.
That does not authorize you to remove, advance, flush, reconnect or
replace one. The current Emergency Medical Assistants Regulation and
employer direction define scope; training must also be current and
supervised.

8
00:02:29,688 --> 00:02:47,736
The medication framework in a nursing procedure text does not replace
BCEHS medication-safety guidance. For nausea, use current E07 and the
applicable drug monograph, and stay within the patient’s indication,
provider level, education and service direction.

9
00:02:47,736 --> 00:03:01,512
For a displaced feeding tube, explain why visible displacement does
not tell you where the internal end is. Identify the patient findings
that increase urgency and the history that should travel with the
handover.

10
00:03:01,512 --> 00:03:17,088
For a catheter with low output, connect the observation to hydration,
renal and systemic illness without diagnosing from urine appearance.
For nausea, consider serious causes before selecting a pathway-based
symptom intervention.
